b"} {"question" "q-72038" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/225e79155fe769efb2273d5063fce38ce305ae25a11a0276e505b08420da790a.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-73532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/d69e6b6550f7e4cae0785daaa19c0a52382a595c40309dccd1d832dbc6d2f82f.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type I region in two-dimensional space?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-73532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{y-1}+1\\,⟹\\,y=(x-1)^{2}+1\n$$\n\nThe function $y=-(x-1)^{2}+3$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=2.$ So, we have $1\\le x\\le 2.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/942a39f885e6f88bef997e27914d05359be86ae1d6204f3834f0829ce4b81136.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=(x-1)^{2}+1$ and leaves through the upper function $y=-(x-1)^{2}+3.$ So, we have $(x-1)^{2}+1\\le y\\le -(x-1)^{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,(x-1)^{2}+1\\le y\\le -(x-1)^{2}+3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-73532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8520f561996cbff88d81d09e9e399101a6c1536c5dacc3d385980dab31a0d4b0"} {"question" "q-73532" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-73532" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-{x}^{2}+3\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1}"] ["math" "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,(x-1)^{2}+3\\le y\\le -(x-1)^{2}+1}"] ["math" "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,(x-1)^{2}+1\\le y\\le -(x-1)^{2}+3}"] ["math" "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le -(x-1)^{2}}"] ["math" "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le -(x-1)^{2}+3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-73532" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,(x-1)^{2}+1\\le y\\le -(x-1)^{2}+3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-168140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-168140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/1bee7870760a7c267ca83806f04b3fa8b933891fa5e5524ce76ee09f2c7c98e0.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-168140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8520f561996cbff88d81d09e9e399101a6c1536c5dacc3d385980dab31a0d4b0"} {"question" "q-168140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-168140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/32e6ed07f3229c8e6016d12dd35c42597888662fbc909f53bc07e495a73958e8.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/36275eef6e634c83c614626bc2af6b97cd5bafddc8c02b64ba2a1aa7ea983317.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/5ac45c6757e187796674ca4beda4ff73c60835b401df302bfd8a91fa5c98b68c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/de643820e7690a10eae2e7de2e2cb974700996b36130af4f45c446640b49dcc4.png"] ["text" "None of the listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-168140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/de643820e7690a10eae2e7de2e2cb974700996b36130af4f45c446640b49dcc4.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-72140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/c72f0a6f9bfb83be34cf911d2a2a18b04a40f92f4d1f6c065b9d6f03f56301b6.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type II region in two-dimensional space?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-72140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2(x-1)^{3}+1\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y-1}{2}}+1\n$$\n\nThe function $x=0$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=3.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le 3.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/1dcfc91a9392998f6de0e4ede2e67d9487f71bb521adad024c3c6891b56d0163.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=0$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y-1}{2}}+1.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y-1}{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y-1}{2}}+1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-72140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8520f561996cbff88d81d09e9e399101a6c1536c5dacc3d385980dab31a0d4b0"} {"question" "q-72140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-72140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y-1}{2}}+1}"] ["math" "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2(x-1)^{3}+1,\\,0\\le x\\le 1}"] ["math" "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2(x-1)^{3}+1,\\,1\\le x\\le 2}"] ["math" "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y-1}{2}}+1}"] ["math" "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y-1}{2}}-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-72140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y-1}{2}}+1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q