ding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ and the line $y=2-|x|?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-72186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/6f35161e7efbd53c9ef558ffe88d8e046771128965a9fdd02143ae37a9b130ea.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,2-|x|\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- The horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/769a521cd6b05ecf05052da382e40295b26be66fe56e4eff7c6940c9032f2f0b.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-72186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8520f561996cbff88d81d09e9e399101a6c1536c5dacc3d385980dab31a0d4b0"} {"question" "q-72186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-72186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "q-72186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3e85eeaf498d17c8a2f26f32fa9b331cf5d99d6c1dbe851af50132414a39f8b"} {"question" "e-6101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/766a7abf73e9f9ef85164b1165166d7493cd36cdc75d0f244e475fed8aeea8fd.png)\n\nRepresent the type I region above using set notation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/example-6101.json" "file_sha256" "043fad317e1ae52d69604aae4fdbe36f28dc4601b79f9537dcba154230cb488b"} {"question" "e-6101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq x  \\leq b, \\quad g(x) \\leq y \\leq f(x) \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x.$\n\n- The curve $x=y-1$ can be written as $y=x+1.$\n\n- The curve $y=7-x$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3.$ So, we have $1 \\leq x \\leq 3.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x)$. A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/acf870e74a9029543a6e6b21995c293f67f251b54dfad9584220780bc3a0b454.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=x+1$ and leaves through the upper function $y=7-x.$ So, we have $x+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x \\big\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/example-6101.json" "file_sha256" "043fad317e1ae52d69604aae4fdbe36f28dc4601b79f9537dcba154230cb488b"} {"question" "e-6102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/3919a6a8442e983a9ca3465b2ed9abbeda18b7dc8d21b7c74b1851b53c1be714.png)\n\nRepresent the type II region above using set notation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/example-6102.json" "file_sha256" "4c835bb9954c1eed65ee6ca22a640c3767c64f41b6fc47e743df50652889cc2a"} {"question" "e-6102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq y  \\leq b, \\quad g(y) \\leq x \\leq f(y) \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y.$\n\n- The curve $y = 2-x^2$ can be written as $x = -\\sqrt{2-y}.$ Note that we have picked the negative square root since $x\\leq 0$ in our region.\n\n- The curve $x=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=-2$ and $y=2.$ So, we have $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y)$. A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/0cf258d0269db0977d5c19fe7abd159f13319bb493394fbd049e5660d88a1d6f.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=-\\sqrt{2-y}$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}.$ So, we have $-\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\bigg\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: -\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2} \\bigg\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/example-6102.json" "file_sha256" "4c835bb9954c1eed65ee6ca22a640c3767c64f41b6fc47e743df50652889cc2a"} {"question" "e-6103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=x^2$ and the line $y=4?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n\n- It is a type II region\n\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/example-6103.json" "file_sha256" "8824e8ed85037822d0b4c4cf27271639b41eb5c25f22b9e5625f736cfacef14e"} {"question" "e-6103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=x^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx = \\pm\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/59d32c44bae848d52adc188e15a8bab470b3eccd887cad45c231b66c486284fc.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -\\sqrt{y} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/example-6103.json" "file_sha256" "8824e8ed85037822d0b4c4cf27271639b41eb5c25f22b9e5625f736cfacef14e"}] "usage" {"e-6102" [] "q-71809" [] "q-71816" [] "e-6103" [] "q-71895" [] "e-6101" []} "basis" 1337 "retractions" [[17592186129775 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71816.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type I region in two-dimensional space?"] [17592186129779 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circles $x^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} = 1$ and $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$?\n\n1. It is a type I region\n2. It is a type II region\n3. It's neither a type I nor a type II region"] [17592186128921 :question/problem "Represent the type I region above using set notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/46d316127c71bdc5d921d5efb296b113.png)"] [17592186128921 :question/worked-solution "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: a \\leq x \\leq b, \\quad g(x) \\leq y \\leq f(x) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x$.\n\n- The curve $x=y-1$ can be written as $y=x+1$.\n- The curve $y=7-x$ is already written as a function of $x$.\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3$. So, we have\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x)$. A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/196068d46324da2557e27acfe2e6f5d5.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function\n\n$$\ny=x+1\n$$\n\nand leaves through the upper function $y=7-x$. So, we have\n\n$$\nx+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x \\big\\}\n$$"] [17592186128922 :question/problem "Represent the type II region above using set notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/527c08bc412d4f54db57900a3bb5d731.png)"] [17592186128922 :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: a \\leq y \\leq b, \\quad g(y) \\leq x \\leq f(y) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y$.\n\n- The curve $y = 2-x^2$ can be written as $x = -\\sqrt{2-y}$. Note that we have picked the negative square root since $x\\leq 0$ in our region.\n- The curve $x=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}$ is already written as a function of $y$.\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=-2$ and $y=2$. So, we have\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y)$. A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/a1e788a35779364579390f7c39e5532d.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function\n\n$$\nx=-\\sqrt{2-y}\n$$\n\nand leaves through the upper function\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\bigg\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: -\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2} \\bigg\\}\n$$"] [17592186128923 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=x^2$ and the line $y=4$?\n\n1. It is a type I region\n2. It is a type II region\n3. It's neither a type I nor a type II region"] [17592186128923 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=x^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = \\pm\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/dc17a2cba3c4044e9c97228407b26379.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}$.\nSo, this is a type I region.\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -\\sqrt{y} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}$.\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186128439 17592186128440 17592186128441 17592186128442 17592186128443 17592186128445 17592186128446 17592186128447 17592186128448 17592186128449 17592186128